{
  "image_name": "0c1056aa3ed8682521e6d8946885e732.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "This schematic illustrates how long-range chromatin looping may regulate **GRIN2B** gene expression. At the top, the gene’s transcription start site (TSS) is shown within non-coding DNA, with distant regulatory regions located roughly **370 kb** and **450 kb** away. In this baseline state, the promoter is not actively engaged and there is no GRIN2B expression.\n\nThe main comparison is between an active and a repressed chromatin configuration. In the active state, the DNA folds so that distant enhancer regions loop into proximity with the TSS. Transcription factors such as **AP-1** and **NRF1** bind these looped regions, and their contact with the promoter appears to stimulate transcription, leading to increased GRIN2B expression.\n\nIn the repressed state, the looping pattern changes. Repressive chromatin-associated factors, including **SETDB1**, **H3K9me3**, and **HP1α**, accumulate near the TSS, while factors bound at the distal region are separated from the promoter, with **CTCF** also shown near the 450 kb site. This arrangement is associated with decreased transcription.\n\nOverall, the figure conveys that GRIN2B expression depends on three-dimensional genome architecture: enhancer-promoter looping promotes transcription, whereas repressive chromatin organization reduces it.",
